			"date": "2024-05-25T16:25:10.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> as much as I know about shorthands, this is exactly how they work: only write spoken form of the syllables* (and many even omit vowels).</p><p>for example, in Gregg, \"wrestle\" looks the same as \"rest\" (which is written as `r` + `s`) + `l` !</p><p>*: that's why every language has their own shorthand. usually a popular shorthand is adapted to conform the given language's pronounciation </p><p>sources:<br>- <a href=\"https://greggdict.rliu.dev/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">greggdict.rliu.dev/</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a> for word lookup<br>- <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/greggshorthand/comments/yyynjk/gregg_simplified_alphabet_card_courtesy_of_the/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">reddit.com/r/greggshorthand/co</span><span class=\"invisible\">mments/yyynjk/gregg_simplified_alphabet_card_courtesy_of_the/</span></a> for alphabet reference</p>",
